// Rep Max Key idea cards: one per topic. Fields: s, t, key (the rule), trap (common mistake), ref (where it sits in the course).
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "key": "Economics is a social science: models simplify reality using assumptions. Ceteris paribus ('all else equal') isolates one change, because economists cannot run controlled experiments on whole economies.", "trap": "Thinking ceteris paribus means other things never change in real life. It is a deliberate assumption to isolate one effect, not a claim about reality.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.1 (Economics as a social science)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "key": "Positive statements can be tested against evidence, so can be true or false. Normative statements contain value judgements (should, fair, better) and cannot be proved or disproved by data alone.", "trap": "Calling a statement positive because it has a number in it, or because it sounds factual. A false claim can still be positive; a normative one rests on opinion.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.2 (Positive and normative statements)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "key": "Scarcity: finite resources, unlimited wants, so choices must be made. Opportunity cost = the value of the next best alternative given up, not the total of all alternatives.", "trap": "Counting every forgone option, or the money price, as the opportunity cost. It is only the next best alternative given up.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.3 (The economic problem)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "key": "Points on the PPF use all resources efficiently; inside = unemployed or inefficient; outside = unattainable. Opportunity cost = sacrificed good ÷ gained good. Outward shift = growth.", "trap": "Saying a movement along the PPF is economic growth. Growth is an outward shift of the whole PPF; a movement along it is a trade-off.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.4 (Production possibility frontiers)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "key": "Specialisation and division of labour raise productivity and output, so surplus can be traded. Money acts as a medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account and standard of deferred payment.", "trap": "Saying division of labour and specialisation are identical, or listing only benefits. Include drawbacks: boredom, dependence on others, lost skills, and the double coincidence of wants under barter.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.5 (Specialisation and division of labour)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "key": "Free market: price mechanism allocates resources, private ownership. Command: state plans and owns. Mixed: both. Smith: invisible hand. Hayek: dispersed knowledge. Marx: collective ownership.", "trap": "Calling the UK a free market. Every real economy is mixed; the question is the degree of state involvement, not whether it exists.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.1.6 (Free market, mixed and command economies)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "key": "Rational economic decision making: consumers maximise utility, firms maximise profit (MR = MC), governments maximise social welfare. Each weighs costs and benefits.", "trap": "Thinking 'rational' means selfish or always correct. It means choosing the best option given the information and aims; behavioural economics shows real choices often differ.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.1 (Rational decision making)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "key": "A price change causes a movement along the demand curve. A change in a condition of demand (income, tastes, related goods' prices, population, advertising, expectations) shifts the curve.", "trap": "Saying a fall in price shifts the demand curve. Price is on the axis, so its change only moves you along the curve.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.2 (Demand)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "key": "PED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP; YED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY; XED = %ΔQd of A ÷ %ΔP of B. Sign matters: YED + normal, − inferior; XED + substitutes, − complements.", "trap": "Using absolute changes instead of percentages, or ignoring the sign. For YED and XED the sign tells you the type of good; PED is usually negative.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.3 (Price, income and cross elasticities of demand)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "key": "A change in the good's own price causes a movement along supply. A change in a condition of supply (costs, technology, taxes, subsidies, number of firms, weather) shifts the curve.", "trap": "Saying a rise in costs 'moves along' the curve. Costs shift it left; only the good's own price causes a movement.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.4 (Supply)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "key": "PES = %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP. It is positive and measures how responsive supply is to a price change. Supply is more elastic with spare capacity, stocks, mobile factors and more time.", "trap": "Using absolute changes instead of percentage changes, or saying PES is negative. Supply slopes upwards, so PES is positive. Short run supply is less elastic than long run.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.5 (Elasticity of supply)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "key": "Equilibrium is where Qd = Qs. Above it: excess supply, price falls. Below it: excess demand, price rises. A shift of D or S moves equilibrium along the other curve.", "trap": "Saying a price change shifts the curve. Only non-price factors shift D or S; a price change is a movement along the curve.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.6 (Price determination)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "key": "Price mechanism: prices ration scarce goods (high price deters demand), give incentives (profit motivates producers) and signal (price changes show where resources are wanted).", "trap": "Saying prices only 'rise and fall'. Name the function (rationing, incentive, signalling) and link it to a shift in demand or supply.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.7 (Price mechanism)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "key": "CS = area under demand, above price. PS = area above supply, below price. Triangle = ½ × quantity × price gap. A shift moves equilibrium, so both areas change.", "trap": "Using the whole price as the triangle's height, or forgetting the ½. Height is the gap between the axis intercept and the market price.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.8 (Consumer and producer surplus)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "key": "A per-unit tax shifts supply up by the tax; a subsidy shifts it down. Consumer burden = rise in price × new Q. Producer burden = (tax − price rise) × new Q. Revenue = tax × new Q.", "trap": "Using the old quantity, or assuming consumers pay the whole tax. Use the new quantity and split the tax by how far the market price rises.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.9 (Indirect taxes and subsidies)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "key": "Behavioural economics: people often depart from rational utility maximising. They follow the herd (social norms), act on habit, and use rules of thumb when calculations are hard, so demand may not follow theory.", "trap": "Calling these choices 'irrational' with no link to demand. Always show how the behaviour changes the quantity bought or the market outcome.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.2.10 (Alternative views of consumer behaviour)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "key": "Market failure: the free market allocates resources inefficiently, so social welfare is not maximised. Causes: externalities (MSB≠MPB or MSC≠MPC), missing public goods, information gaps.", "trap": "Saying a good is a demerit good or harmful is not enough. Market failure needs a gap between private and social costs or benefits, or a missing market.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.3.1 (Types of market failure)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "key": "Social cost = private + external cost; social benefit = private + external benefit. Free markets set MPB=MPC, not MSB=MSC, so the welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB.", "trap": "Drawing welfare loss as a rectangle or the total external cost; it is only the triangle between MSC and MSB, from the social optimum to market output.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.3.2 (Externalities)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "key": "Public goods are non-rival (one person's use doesn't reduce others') and non-excludable (can't stop non-payers). Free riders mean no price can be charged, so markets under-provide or fail to provide.", "trap": "Calling any government-provided good a public good. Schools and the NHS are publicly provided but are rival or excludable, so they are not pure public goods.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.3.3 (Public goods)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "key": "Asymmetric information: one party (buyer or seller) knows more than the other. Decisions are made on poor information, so price signals mislead and resources are misallocated: market failure.", "trap": "Saying information gaps only affect buyers. Sellers can be less informed too (e.g. insurers), and symmetric information means both sides equally informed, not perfectly informed.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.3.4 (Information gaps)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "key": "A per-unit tax shifts supply left (up) by the tax; a subsidy shifts it right (down). Incidence depends on PED and PES: the less elastic side bears more of the tax.", "trap": "Assuming consumers pay the whole tax. The price rise is less than the tax unless demand is perfectly inelastic or supply perfectly elastic; the burden is split.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.4.1 (Government intervention in markets)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "key": "Government failure: intervention causes a net welfare loss, so resources are allocated less efficiently than without it. Causes: unintended consequences, distorted price signals, admin costs, information gaps.", "trap": "Saying government failure means a policy missed its target. It must cause a net welfare loss compared with the outcome without intervention.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 1.4.2 (Government failure)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "key": "Economic growth is the percentage rise in real GDP: $\\text{Real GDP}=\\frac{\\text{Nominal GDP}}{\\text{Price index}}\\times 100$. Divide by population for output per head.", "trap": "Using nominal GDP confuses inflation with extra output, and total GDP ignores population growth, inequality and unpaid work.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.1.1 (Economic growth)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "key": "Inflation = sustained rise in the general price level (CPI, weighted basket). Rate = change in index ÷ old index × 100. Disinflation: prices rise more slowly. Deflation: prices fall.", "trap": "Calling a fall in the inflation rate 'deflation'. Falling from 4% to 2% is disinflation; prices still rise. Deflation needs a negative inflation rate.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.1.2 (Inflation)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "key": "Unemployment is measured by the Labour Force Survey (ILO: seeking and available for work) and the Claimant Count (benefit claimants). Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force × 100.", "trap": "Economically inactive people (students, retired, carers not seeking work) are outside the labour force, so they are not counted as unemployed.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.1.3 (Employment and unemployment)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "key": "Current account = trade in goods + trade in services + primary income + secondary income. A deficit means total outflows on these four items exceed total inflows.", "trap": "Do not treat the current account as just exports minus imports: primary income (investment flows) and secondary income (transfers) are also included.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.1.4 (Balance of payments)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "key": "Aggregate demand is total spending at a given price level: $AD=C+I+G+(X-M)$. Price-level changes move along AD; non-price changes in any component shift AD.", "trap": "A fall in the general price level causes an extension down along the AD curve, never a rightward shift of the AD curve.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.2.1 (The characteristics of AD)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "key": "Consumption depends mainly on disposable income, plus interest rates, confidence and wealth. $MPC=\\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y}$, $APC=\\frac{C}{Y}$, and $MPC+MPS=1$.", "trap": "Do not mix up income (a flow per period) with wealth (a stock of assets), or confuse MPC (change) with APC (total share).", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.2.2 (Consumption)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "key": "Investment is firms' spending on capital goods. $\\text{Net investment}=\\text{Gross investment}-\\text{Depreciation}$. Drivers: growth, interest rates, credit, confidence, animal spirits.", "trap": "In economics, investment means buying physical capital goods (machinery, buildings), never putting money into a bank account or buying existing shares.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.2.3 (Investment)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "key": "Government expenditure ($G$) changes automatically across the trade cycle (automatic stabilisers) and deliberately through discretionary fiscal policy.", "trap": "Transfer payments like state pensions and unemployment benefits are not in $G$ in AD. They enter AD only when households spend them as $C$.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.2.4 (Government expenditure)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "key": "Net trade (X−M) depends on UK and world real incomes, the sterling exchange rate (SPICED), protectionism and non-price competitiveness. A stronger pound tends to worsen it.", "trap": "A weaker pound need not improve (X−M) at once: in the short run demand for exports and imports is price-inelastic, so dearer imports can worsen it.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.2.5 (Net trade)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.1", "key": "A change in the price level moves you along SRAS. A change in costs shifts SRAS. A change in the quantity or quality of factors, or in productivity, shifts LRAS (potential output).", "trap": "Saying a rise in the price level shifts SRAS right. It is a movement along SRAS; only non-price-level factors such as costs shift the curve.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.3.1 (The characteristics of AS)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.2", "key": "Higher costs (raw materials, energy, imported inputs after a weaker £, business taxes) shift SRAS left. Lower costs, a stronger £ or lower business taxes shift it right.", "trap": "Showing a cost change as a movement along SRAS. Only a change in the general price level moves along the curve; a change in costs shifts the whole curve.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.3.2 (Short-run AS)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.3.3", "key": "LRAS shows potential output with all resources fully employed. Classical: vertical. Keynesian: flat, then rising, then vertical. It shifts right with more or better factors of production.", "trap": "Treating a rise in AD as a shift of LRAS. AD changes move along a Keynesian LRAS but never shift it; only supply-side changes shift it.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.3.3 (Long-run AS)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.1", "key": "Circular flow: injections (J = I + G + X) enter, withdrawals (W = S + T + M) leave. Income is a flow per period; wealth is a stock at a point in time. Equilibrium: J = W.", "trap": "Treating wealth and income as the same thing, or thinking saving simply disappears. Saving is a withdrawal; wealth is the stock built up from past saving.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.4.1 (National income: circular flow of income; income and wealth)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.2", "key": "Injections (I, G, X) add to the circular flow; withdrawals (S, T, M) take income out. If J > W national income rises; if J < W it falls. Equilibrium: I + G + X = S + T + M.", "trap": "Confusing saving with investment. Saving (S) is a withdrawal; investment (I) is an injection. Imports are a withdrawal, not an injection.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.4.2 (Injections and withdrawals)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.3", "key": "Short-run equilibrium output is where AD = SRAS. AD right: output and prices rise. SRAS left: output falls, prices rise. On a vertical (classical) LRAS, AD changes only alter the price level.", "trap": "Mixing up the curves: higher costs shift SRAS left, not AD. Also, a move along a curve after a shift is not a second shift.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.4.3 (Equilibrium national output)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "key": "Multiplier k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM). Final change in income = k × initial injection. MPS, MPT and MPM are the shares of extra income withdrawn.", "trap": "Leaving out tax or imports from the withdrawals, or stopping at k. Multiply k by the injection. MPW 0.25 gives k = 4, not 0.25.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.4.4 (The multiplier)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.1", "key": "Actual growth = rise in real GDP (AD shifts right, or a move towards the PPF). Potential growth = rise in productive capacity (LRAS or PPF shifts out) from more or better factors of production.", "trap": "Treating a rise in AD as raising potential output. Higher demand gives actual growth only; potential growth needs the LRAS or PPF to shift outwards.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.5.1 (Causes of growth)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.2", "key": "Output gap = actual output − potential output. As a % of potential: (Y − Y*) ÷ Y* × 100. Positive: actual above potential (inflation pressure). Negative: spare capacity.", "trap": "Dividing by actual GDP instead of potential, or treating a positive gap as purely good. It is above sustainable output, so inflation pressure builds.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.5.2 (Output gaps)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.3", "key": "Trade cycle: actual GDP fluctuates around trend. Boom: fast growth, falling unemployment, rising inflation. Recession: real GDP falls for 2+ quarters, unemployment rises, inflation falls.", "trap": "Calling any slowdown a recession. Slower but still positive growth is not a recession; a technical recession needs real GDP to fall for two consecutive quarters.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.5.3 (Trade (business) cycle)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.5.4", "key": "Growth raises incomes, profits, tax revenue and living standards, but can bring inequality, inflation, resource depletion and pollution. Net effect depends on the type of growth and who gains.", "trap": "Assuming higher real GDP per head means everyone is better off. It is an average: it ignores distribution, environmental damage and non-market costs.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.5.4 (The impact of economic growth)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.1", "key": "Objectives: growth, low unemployment, low stable inflation (2% CPI target), current account balance, balanced budget, environment, equality (Gini). Each has its own measure and they can conflict.", "trap": "Mixing up the current account with the government budget, or reading a higher Gini coefficient as more equal. Higher Gini means more inequality.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.6.1 (Possible macroeconomic objectives)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "key": "Demand-side policy shifts AD. Monetary: Bank Rate, QE, set by the MPC (2% CPI target). Fiscal: G, T, borrowing. Lower rates, higher G or lower T raise AD; the reverse reduces it.", "trap": "Mixing up the tools: the MPC sets Bank Rate and QE, but the government sets taxes and spending. Also assuming a cut in rates always lifts AD.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.6.2 (Demand-side policies)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.3", "key": "Supply-side policies aim to raise productive capacity, shifting LRAS right. Market-based: incentives, competition, flexibility. Interventionist: government spends on skills, infrastructure, R&D.", "trap": "Calling tax cuts that boost spending 'supply-side'. Only policies that raise potential output count, and they work slowly, with time lags.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.6.3 (Supply-side policies)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.4", "key": "Objectives can conflict: a policy that helps one can harm another. Short-run Phillips curve: lower unemployment comes with higher inflation. It shifts right if expectations rise or SRAS falls.", "trap": "Treating the Phillips curve as a permanent trade-off, or calling a shift a movement along it. Shifts come from expectations or supply shocks.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 2.6.4 (Conflicts and trade-offs)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.1", "key": "Firms grow for economies of scale, market power and spreading risk; some stay small by choice or constraint. Principal-agent: owners (principals) want profit, managers (agents) may not.", "trap": "Thinking the principal-agent problem is workers v managers. It is shareholders (owners) v managers, caused by the separation of ownership and control.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.1.1 (Sizes and types of firms)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.2", "key": "Organic = growth from own resources. Integration = merger/takeover: horizontal (same stage, same industry), vertical forward (towards customer), backward (towards supplier), conglomerate (unrelated).", "trap": "Mixing up forward and backward vertical integration: forward is towards the customer or retail, backward is towards the supplier or raw materials.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.1.2 (Business growth)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.1.3", "key": "A demerger splits one firm into separate firms. Reasons: remove diseconomies of scale, regain focus, release value. Costs: lost economies of scale, job losses. Effects depend on context.", "trap": "Confusing a demerger with a takeover or share sale, or assuming it always cuts costs and prices. It can also lose economies of scale.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.1.3 (Demergers)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.2.1", "key": "Profit max: MC = MR (MC cutting MR from below). Revenue max: MR = 0. Sales max: highest output where AR = AC (normal profit). Satisficing: just enough profit to keep shareholders content.", "trap": "Using MC = MR for every objective. Revenue maximisation needs MR = 0 and sales maximisation needs AR = AC; MC does not set those outputs.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.2.1 (Business objectives)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.1", "key": "TR = P × Q; AR = TR ÷ Q (= price); MR = ΔTR ÷ ΔQ. With a linear demand curve, MR lies below AR and is twice as steep. Elastic demand: a price cut raises TR. Inelastic: it lowers TR.", "trap": "Treating MR as the price, or assuming TR always rises when price falls. MR is the extra revenue from one more unit, and it can be negative.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.3.1 (Revenue)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.2", "key": "TC = FC + VC; AC = TC/Q; MC = ΔTC/ΔQ. MC cuts AVC and ATC at their minimum points. Short-run U-shape: diminishing returns. Long-run U-shape: economies then diseconomies of scale.", "trap": "Mixing the two U-shapes: short-run costs rise from diminishing returns; long-run costs rise from diseconomies of scale. Fixed costs never affect MC.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.3.2 (Costs)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.3", "key": "Internal economies of scale fall LRAC as a firm grows (technical, purchasing, financial, managerial, marketing, risk-bearing). Minimum efficient scale (MES) is the lowest output at which LRAC is at its minimum.", "trap": "Mixing up internal and external: external economies come from the industry's size (a cluster, shared suppliers), not from one firm growing. Also, diseconomies raise LRAC.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.3.3 (Economies and diseconomies of scale)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "key": "Profit is maximised where MC = MR. Supernormal profit: AR > AC, area (AR − AC) × Q. Normal profit: AR = AC. Short-run shutdown if AR < AVC; long-run exit if AR < AC.", "trap": "Shutting down as soon as AR < AC. In the short run a loss-making firm should keep producing while AR ≥ AVC, because it still covers some fixed costs.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.3.4 (Normal and supernormal profits and losses)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.1", "key": "Allocative efficiency: P = MC. Productive efficiency: output at the lowest point of AC (MC = AC). Dynamic: investment and innovation lower long-run costs. X-inefficiency: costs above the minimum.", "trap": "Saying perfect competition is efficient in every way. It is allocatively and productively efficient in the long run, but it is not dynamically efficient.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.1 (Efficiency)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.2", "key": "Price taker: D=AR=MR is horizontal. Produce where MC=MR. Short run: supernormal profit, normal profit or loss. Long run: free entry and exit leave normal profit, with P=MC=minimum AC.", "trap": "Thinking a firm shuts down whenever it makes a loss. In the short run it stays open if AR≥AVC. Also, supernormal profit does not last in the long run.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.2 (Perfect competition)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.3", "key": "Many firms, differentiated products, free entry. SR: MR=MC, supernormal profit or loss. LR: entry/exit shifts AR until AR=AC (normal profit), with P>MC and output below minimum AC.", "trap": "Saying long-run monopolistic competition is efficient like perfect competition. It is not: output is below minimum AC (excess capacity) and P>MC.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.3 (Monopolistic competition)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "key": "Oligopoly = few large, interdependent firms with high barriers to entry. n-firm concentration ratio = combined market share of top n firms. Collusion lifts profit, but each firm gains by cheating.", "trap": "Dividing the top firms' sales by the number of firms, or by the wrong total, instead of dividing by total market sales and multiplying by 100.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.4 (Oligopoly)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.5", "key": "A profit-maximising monopolist produces where MR = MC, then charges the price on the AR (demand) curve at that output. Because P > MC, it is allocatively inefficient.", "trap": "Reading the price off the MR or MC curve at MR = MC. Go up to the AR (demand) curve to find the price.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.5 (Monopoly)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.6", "key": "Monopsony: one dominant buyer. It hires where MRP = MC of labour, but pays the wage read off the supply (AC) curve below that point. So wage and employment are lower than in competition.", "trap": "Reading the wage off the MC or MRP curve at the chosen quantity. Go down to the supply (AC) curve: that is the wage actually paid.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.6 (Monopsony)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.7", "key": "A contestable market has low barriers to entry and exit and low sunk costs, so the threat of hit-and-run entry disciplines incumbents even if the market has few firms.", "trap": "Assuming few firms means no competition. Contestability depends on the threat of entry, not the number of firms now in the market.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.4.7 (Contestability)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.5.1", "key": "Labour demand is a derived demand: firms hire workers for the goods they produce. The MRP curve is the demand curve; MRP = MPP × MR (price if perfect competition).", "trap": "Treating a wage change as a shift in labour demand. A wage change moves along MRP; changes in productivity or product price or demand shift it.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.5.1 (Demand for labour)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.5.2", "key": "Labour supply to a firm/occupation slopes upward: higher wages attract more workers. Non-wage factors shift it. Immobility (geographical, occupational) limits supply and keeps unemployment alongside vacancies.", "trap": "Confusing a wage change (movement along supply) with a change in non-wage factors such as qualifications, migration or benefits (shift of supply).", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.5.2 (Supply of labour)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.5.3", "key": "Competitive market: wage is set where labour demand (MRP) = labour supply. A binding minimum wage creates excess labour supply, smaller if demand is inelastic. Under monopsony, jobs can rise.", "trap": "Saying a minimum wage always cuts jobs. It only bites if set above equilibrium, and with a monopsony employer it can raise both wages and employment.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.5.3 (Wage determination)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.6.1", "key": "Government limits market power: the CMA can clear, block or add remedies to mergers, regulators cap prices (RPI−X) and open markets up. Judge benefits against costs and the risk of government failure.", "trap": "Thinking the CMA simply blocks big mergers. It can clear them or accept remedies, and regulation can fail too (for example, poor information).", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.6.1 (Government intervention)"}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.6.2", "key": "Show the effect on price, quantity, profit, quality and choice (shortage or surplus for price controls; elasticity for taxes and subsidies), then weigh unintended effects and costs.", "trap": "Saying intervention simply 'fixes' the market. Price controls create shortages or surpluses and risk government failure, so always give a limit.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 3.6.2 (The impact of government intervention)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.1", "key": "Globalisation is growing economic integration of countries via trade, capital, labour and information flows. Drivers: trade liberalisation, TNCs, technology, transport, falling costs. Effects differ by group.", "trap": "Treating globalisation as good for everyone. Gains (lower prices, growth, FDI) come with losers: structural unemployment, wage pressure, inequality, environmental damage.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.1 (Globalisation)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.2", "key": "Comparative advantage = lower opportunity cost. OC of X = units of Y given up ÷ units of X gained. Specialise where OC is lower and trade at a price between the two OCs: both gain.", "trap": "Using absolute output to decide who specialises. Compare opportunity costs: even a country worse at everything has a comparative advantage.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.2 (Specialisation and trade)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.3", "key": "Trade patterns shift as opportunity costs, emerging-economy growth, trading blocs and exchange rates change. Comparative advantage means lower opportunity cost, not just cheaper or more output.", "trap": "Mixing up absolute and comparative advantage: a country best at making everything still gains by specialising where its opportunity cost is lower.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.3 (Pattern of trade)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.4", "key": "Terms of trade = (export price index ÷ import price index) × 100. A rise is an improvement: each unit of exports buys more imports. Import prices rising faster than export prices worsens it.", "trap": "Inverting the formula (imports ÷ exports), or assuming a higher terms of trade always improves the current account. Export volumes and elasticity matter too.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.4 (Terms of trade)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.5", "key": "Free trade area: no internal tariffs, each member sets its own external tariffs. Customs union adds a common external tariff. Common market adds free movement of factors. Weigh creation vs diversion.", "trap": "Assuming blocs always gain: trade diversion switches buying from cheaper non-members to dearer members. Also mixing up a free trade area and a customs union.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.5 (Trading blocs and the WTO)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.6", "key": "Tariffs and quotas raise the domestic price, cut imports and protect home producers. Tariff revenue goes to government; under a quota, importers keep the extra price. Welfare is lost overall.", "trap": "Saying protection helps everyone. Producers (and government, for tariffs) gain, but consumers lose, deadweight loss arises and partners may retaliate.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.6 (Restrictions on free trade)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.7", "key": "Current account = trade in goods and services + primary income + secondary income. A deficit is matched by net inflows on the capital and financial accounts. Fix by expenditure switching, reducing or supply-side policy.", "trap": "Thinking a current account deficit means the balance of payments doesn't balance. In total it always does: the financial account (plus capital account) offsets the deficit.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.7 (Balance of payments)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "key": "Floating: demand and supply set the rate. Fixed: the central bank holds it by buying its currency with reserves and raising interest rates. Devaluation = deliberate cut in a fixed rate. J-curve: balance worsens, then improves.", "trap": "Mixing up depreciation (floating, market-driven) with devaluation (fixed, government decision), and assuming a weaker currency improves the current account immediately.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.8 (Exchange rates)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.9", "key": "Unit labour cost = total labour cost ÷ output. It rises if pay grows faster than productivity. Higher relative ULC or relative export prices (in a common currency) reduce price competitiveness.", "trap": "Assuming higher wages always make a country less competitive. What matters is pay relative to productivity, and relative to rivals' costs and the exchange rate.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.1.9 (International competitiveness)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.2.1", "key": "Absolute poverty: income below a fixed real threshold for basic needs. Relative poverty: income below 60% of median household income, so the line moves when median income changes.", "trap": "Assuming growth always cuts relative poverty. If all incomes rise by the same %, the median and the 60% line rise too, so relative poverty is unchanged.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.2.1 (Absolute and relative poverty)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "key": "Lorenz curve: cumulative % of income against cumulative % of households. The further it bows from the 45° line, the more unequal. Gini = A ÷ (A+B): 0 is equality, 1 is total inequality.", "trap": "A higher Gini means MORE inequality, not less. Also, wealth is a stock of assets, while income is a flow; don't mix them up.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.2.2 (Inequality)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.1", "key": "HDI (0 to 1) combines life expectancy, mean and expected years of schooling, and GNI per capita (PPP). It is broader than GDP, but it is an average that ignores inequality and sustainability.", "trap": "Assuming HDI includes inequality, the environment or growth. It does not, and a high HDI does not show that income is shared fairly.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.3.1 (Measures of development)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.2", "key": "Barriers to growth: primary product dependence (volatile prices, low value added, Prebisch-Singer), savings gap (Harrod-Domar), foreign currency gap, and debt (debt service crowds out spending).", "trap": "Saying primary products always mean low growth. The problem is dependence on a narrow range, with volatile prices and weak terms of trade, not exporting primaries itself.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.3.2 (Factors influencing growth and development)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.3.3", "key": "Market-based: free trade, FDI, privatisation. Interventionist: state spending on human capital/infrastructure, tariffs, aid. IMF = short-term loans for stability; World Bank = long-term development finance.", "trap": "Mixing up the IMF (short-term loans, conditions) with the World Bank (long-term projects), or labelling state-funded infrastructure or aid as market-based.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.3.3 (Strategies for growth and development)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.1", "key": "Financial markets link savers to borrowers, allow currency exchange, let firms hedge price and currency risk through forward contracts, and let firms raise funds by issuing equities (shares).", "trap": "Thinking a firm receives money whenever its shares are traded. Only a new issue (primary market) raises funds for the firm; secondary trades pay the seller.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.4.1 (Role of financial markets)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.2", "key": "Financial markets fail through asymmetric information (one side knows more), moral hazard (risk-taking once protected from costs), speculative bubbles and market rigging. Each misallocates resources.", "trap": "Confusing asymmetric information with moral hazard: asymmetry is unequal knowledge; moral hazard is riskier behaviour after risk is shifted onto others.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.4.2 (Market failure in the financial sector)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.4.3", "key": "The Bank of England sets monetary policy (Bank Rate, 2% CPI target), banks the government and commercial banks, lends emergency liquidity as lender of last resort, and regulates the financial system.", "trap": "Mixing up roles: Bank Rate is monetary policy, not regulation, and lender of last resort lends to banks in a liquidity crisis, not to firms or households.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.4.3 (Role of central banks)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.1", "key": "Capital = assets (roads); current = day-to-day running costs; transfers = benefits, no output in return. Crowding out: govt borrowing pushes up interest rates, cutting private investment.", "trap": "Treating transfer payments as government purchases of output. They add to AD only when recipients spend them, so they are not direct spending on goods.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.5.1 (Public expenditure)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.2", "key": "Progressive: average tax rate (tax ÷ income × 100) rises with income. Proportional: constant. Regressive: falls. Laffer curve: above the optimum tax rate, a higher rate lowers revenue.", "trap": "Judging by £ paid, not % of income. A tax can take more £ from the rich yet be regressive if it takes a smaller share of their income.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.5.2 (Taxation)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.3", "key": "Deficit = one year's borrowing (flow); debt = total owed (stock). Actual deficit = structural + cyclical. Automatic stabilisers change tax and welfare with the cycle, with no new decision.", "trap": "Thinking a smaller deficit means falling debt. Debt only falls if there is a surplus. A recession deficit is also not all policy: part of it is cyclical.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.5.3 (Public sector finances)"}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.5.4", "key": "Transfer pricing: a multinational sets prices inside its group to book profit in low-tax countries. Globalisation, mobile capital and tax competition limit national policy; coordination helps.", "trap": "Thinking transfer pricing is just moving a head office. It is mispricing trade inside the group to shift profit, so less tax is paid where real activity happens.", "ref": "Edexcel 9EC0 4.5.4 (Macroeconomic policies in a global context)"}
